  "name": null,
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x$ for $x<-4,$ compute the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, comparing\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}\n$$\n\nwith the formula for the instantaneous rate of change,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to \\,a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}={f}^{′}(-5).\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative of $f$ for $x<-1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x+3,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-5)=2(-5)+3=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}=-7.\n$$",
